From 72727448eb003c4fc22ce543e8f3ff1b3c041302 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chris <meischris39@hotmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 15:08:50 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] RetroPlayer: Run the game in step with the screen

The game loop runs each frame on a timer at the game's own rate, which
is never quite the screen's: a Mega Drive game runs at 59.92 Hz on a
60 Hz display. A finished frame waits up to a whole refresh for the
screen to take it, and as the two drift a frame is shown twice every
few seconds.

Where the game's rate is close to the screen's, run one frame per
refresh, started so that it finishes just before the screen takes it.
The screen's rate comes from the display's reference clock, which now
runs for games whether or not videos are synced to the display, and
which drops out while the display changes mode; the game is then timed
as before, as it is for fast-forward, rewind and rates too far from the
screen's. Pausing stops it until the game runs again. Settings under
Games > General turn it off and set how far a game's speed may change,
2% by default; the margin is <games><pacingmargin> in
advancedsettings.xml.

The sound is resampled by the game's speed, and corrected by up to 0.5%
around that to hold the whole delay where it settled once the sound
started, rather than letting the drift between the game's clock and the
sound card's build up until the sound is flushed or the sink runs dry.
The flush is measured from the sink's latency, so a sink with a long
buffer isn't flushed constantly. The pacing log reports the speed, the
correction and the delay.

ActiveAE needed fixes for the resampling to land. Compensation of less
than a sample per call was dropped; it is now carried over, with the
input it belongs to, until there is a whole sample, and a call too
short to set any leaves the compensation under way alone. It was spread
over the uncompensated sample count, so a speed-up fell short of the
requested rate. And a stream keeps its ratio when its buffers are
rebuilt for a new sink, as the ratio is only sent when it changes.
